  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 110 PM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nTuesday evening through Sunday:\n\nDeep low pressure over Hudson Bay Canada will drag a SW to NE \noriented cold front into the Midwest on Tuesday. The front should \nbisect WI by Tuesday afternoon, with ensemble output for CAPE south \nof the boundary generally between 1,000 and 2,000 joules (locally \nhigher) and a downward trend of instability later in the evening \ninto the overnight hours. 500mb flow along the front should be out \nof the WSW around 30kt, closer to the instability axis SW around \n20kt, keeping the effective bulk shear under 35 kt. With the \nfrontal convection and shear generally arriving too late to tap \ninto the full compliment of the diurnal instability, no \norganized severe weather threat is expected. 20-40% chances for\nshowers and weak thunderstorms reach the northwest half of the \nCWA Tuesday evening, with more widespread 65-75% chances \nholding off until late Tuesday night through Wednesday as the \nright-entrance of an upper jet streak gives convection a boost.\nWind shear may increase to levels that permit strong to severe \nstorms (40-50 kt) Wednesday afternoon and evening, with some PVA\nahead of an approaching shortwave trough timed to arrive in the\nevening. Despite the efforts of the shear and trigger \nmechanism, the broad synoptic lift and overnight / morning \nfrontal convection could keep the skies mostly cloudy on \nWednesday and limit instability.\n\nPrecip chances (30-60%) continue into Wednesday night along the\nfrontal zone in southern WI. Post-dawn Thursday morning through\nFriday is now looking mostly dry as high pressure builds into \nthe region. Deepening low pressure over the northern Great \nPlains would then allow precip chances and slightly above normal\ntemperatures (perhaps highs in the mid 80s and modestly humid) \nto return to our region for the weekend.\n\nSheppard",
